**Read:** [Introduction to Engineering Thermodynamics — Claire Yu Yan](../sources/Introduction-to-Engineering-Thermodynamics-Yan.lingua#r_6AX93SRMGE0XXJTQ1NW6X9QXTW), chapter 5 in full; [Engineering Thermodynamics — Olivier Cleynen](../sources/Engineering-Thermodynamics-Cleynen.lingua#r_38CM7HKS0GSN5T09YDXFKHRVGQ), chapter 3 in full; [Thermodynamics and Chemistry — Howard DeVoe](../sources/Thermodynamics-and-Chemistry-DeVoe.lingua#r_2YHHGY26Y8X9MS9DB3MXZHD1D1), open-system energy and matter.

The shared balance reasoning extends to flowing energy. For a fixed control volume, accumulation equals heat input minus work output plus incoming minus outgoing mass-flow contributions of h + v²/2 + gz. Use a distinct symbol for velocity so it is not confused with specific volume. The formula assumes a consistent description of ports and work terms.

Enthalpy includes the pressure-volume contribution associated with carrying matter through a boundary. Derive this from a moving parcel rather than memorizing an unexplained h in the flow balance. Mass and species conservation remain necessary alongside energy.

Compare startup filling with steady flow. A tank receiving hot fluid can change pressure, temperature and inventory simultaneously; a steady-flow equation cannot describe its entire startup. Check port temperature/pressure profiles and averaging assumptions before replacing a distributed inlet with one number.

